---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import MergePdf from '../islands/MergePdf';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'How many PDFs can I merge at once?', a: 'There’s no fixed limit. Because merging happens on your device, the practical limit is your device’s memory. Dozens of normal documents are no problem, even on a phone.' },
  { q: 'Can I change the order of the files?', a: 'Yes. Drag the cards on a computer, or press and hold a card and then drag it on a phone. You can also use the arrow buttons under each card.' },
  { q: 'Will merging reduce the quality?', a: 'No. Pages are copied exactly as they are, so text stays selectable and images keep their original quality.' },
  { q: 'Can I merge password-protected PDFs?', a: 'Only after removing the password. Open the file with its password in your PDF reader, save an unprotected copy, and add that copy.' },
  { q: 'Are my files uploaded to a server?', a: 'No. PDFSherpa merges the files inside your browser. They never leave your device, and the tool works offline after your first visit.' },
  { q: 'Can I merge just some pages from each file?', a: 'Merge the files first, then open the result in <a href="/organize-pdf">Organize PDF</a> to delete or reorder individual pages.' },
];
---
<ToolLayout
  slug="merge-pdf"
  title="Merge PDF Files Online Free – Combine PDFs | PDFSherpa"
  description="Combine multiple PDFs into one file in seconds. Drag to reorder, then merge right in your browser. No upload, no signup, no watermark."
  h1="Merge PDF"
  lead="Combine several PDFs into one document in seconds. Add your files, drag them into order, and merge. Everything happens on your device."
  howToTitle="How to Merge PDF Files"
  steps={['Select or drop two or more PDFs.', 'Drag the files into the order you want.', 'Click <strong>Merge</strong> and download your combined PDF.']}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={['split-pdf', 'organize-pdf', 'compress-pdf', 'jpg-to-pdf']}
>
  <MergePdf client:load />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>What does Merge PDF do?</h2>
    <p>
      Merge PDF joins several PDF documents into a single file, in the order you choose. It’s the quickest way to send one attachment
      instead of five, combine scanned pages into a single application, or put a cover letter, CV and certificates together for an upload portal.
    </p>
    <p>
      PDFSherpa copies each page exactly as it is. Nothing is re-compressed or re-rendered, so text stays sharp and searchable,
      links keep working, and the merged file is roughly the sum of the originals.
    </p>

    <h2>Step by step</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Add your files.</strong> Tap <em>Select PDFs</em> or drop them on the box. You can add more at any time.</li>
      <li><strong>Arrange them.</strong> Each card shows the first page, the file name and the page count. Drag a card to move it. On phones, press and hold first. The numbered badge shows the final order.</li>
      <li><strong>Merge and download.</strong> The combined PDF is created instantly in your browser. It’s named after your first file, for example <code>contract-merged.pdf</code>.</li>
    </ol>

    <AdSlot variant="inline" slot="tool-article" />

    <h2>Examples</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Job applications:</strong> merge your CV, cover letter and certificates into one PDF when the form allows only a single upload.</li>
      <li><strong>Scanned paperwork:</strong> phone scanners often save one PDF per page. Merge them back into one document.</li>
      <li><strong>Reports:</strong> combine a title page, the main report and appendices exported from different apps.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If the merged file is too big for an upload limit, run it through <a href="/compress-pdf">Compress PDF</a> next. Most scanned documents shrink by more than half.
      On a phone? Read <a href="/guides/merge-pdf-on-phone">how to merge PDF files on your phone</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <p>
      Password-protected or restricted PDFs can’t be merged until you save an unprotected copy. Interactive form fields from different files
      may conflict if they share the same names. Very large batches are limited by your device’s memory rather than by PDFSherpa.
    </p>

    <h2>Private by design</h2>
    <p>
      Most online mergers upload every file to a server. PDFSherpa runs the whole merge inside your browser with the open-source pdf-lib library,
      so your documents are never transmitted, stored or seen by anyone. Once the page has loaded, you can even switch off Wi-Fi and it keeps working.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
